    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d104">

                <pb facs="0195" n="97" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg97"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002 Manchuria/90: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-06-22T16:00:00+08:00">June 22, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 22—7:32 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>689. Following from American Consul General at Mukden: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 21. Referring to my telegram of June 3, 5 p.m. and the published
                            declaration by Manchukuo of June 18th<anchor n="61" xml:id="d104-f1"/>
                            [in] which they propose the taking over [of] the customs in Manchuria
                            and the disruption of the existing administration unless Nanking agrees
                            to hand over the surplus revenue of all Manchurian customs stations, it
                            is understood that the remittance of Dairen customs funds to Shanghai
                            was stopped a few days ago. These funds are held in local banks as is
                            the case at Antung and Newchwang.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d104-f1" n="61" xml:id="d104-f1-content">
                <p>For text of declaration, see telegram No. 704, June 24, 1 p.m., from the Minister
                    in China, <ref target="#pg104">p. 104</ref>.</p>
            </note>
